# Output
Based on my fetches and analysis, here is my structured output:

---

**LINK 1: `https://www.contentstack.com/docs/headless-cms/live-preview-implementation-for-nextjs-csr-app-router`**

- **Classification:** Vendor documentation (Contentstack)
- **Relevance:** RELEVANT — This is the primary implementation guide for the exact feature this ticket builds: Contentstack Live Preview in a Next.js App Router application.
- **Fetched:** Yes (HTTP 200)

**Architecture scope:** The guide is explicitly scoped to **Client-Side Rendering (CSR) with the Next.js App Router**. The doc states: *"Client-side rendering (CSR) means your website loads data and builds pages directly inside the browser using JavaScript. This guide walks you through setting up Contentstack Live Preview for CSR websites using REST APIs."* The `init()` config hardcodes `ssr: false`. I was unable to verify the codeline's architecture (package.json did not contain "contentstack"; next.config files were found but not read due to tool budget). **If this project uses SSR or the Pages Router, this guide is the wrong variant and following it literally would be wrong.**

**CONTRADICTION with story assumption — "no code changes needed":**

The comment by V-NandaKumar KR states: *"Its possible, no code changes are needed and its more of configure and use."*

The documentation **contradicts this**. Code changes are required in multiple places:

1. **Delivery SDK configuration** — The `Contentstack.Stack()` method must be updated with a `live_preview` config block:
   > ```
   > live_preview: {
   >   preview_token: preview_token,
   >   enable: true,
   >   host: 'rest-preview.contentstack.com'
   > },
   > ```

2. **A separate SDK must be installed and initialized** — `npm install @contentstack/live-preview-utils@contentstack/utils`, then:
   > ```
   > ContentstackLivePreview.init({
   >   stackSdk: Stack,
   >   enable: true,
   >   ssr: false,
   >   editButton: { enable: true },
   >   stackDetails: { apiKey: "your api key", environment: "your environment value", branch: "your branch" },
   >   clientUrlParams: { protocol: "https", host: "app.contentstack.com", port: 443 },
   > });
   > ```

3. **Each page component must wire the `onEntryChange` callback** — The callback **takes one argument** (the update function):
   > ```
   > React.useEffect(() => {
   >   onEntryChange(updateData);
   > }, []);
   > ```

4. **Stack settings must also be configured in the Contentstack UI** (Settings > Environments for base URLs; Visual Experience > Enable Live Preview checkbox; Default Preview Environment). So the work is **both code and configuration**, not configuration alone.

**On "Visual experience" tab assumption:** The comment thread states *"The functionality is available only through the 'Visual experience' tab in Contentstack."* The docs confirm that Live Preview is **enabled** via Settings > Visual Experience, but the **implementation requires code changes** as shown above. The Visual Experience tab is where you toggle the feature on and set the preview environment — it is not the only place work happens.

**On `onEntryChange` signature:** The document shows `onEntryChange(updateData)` — the callback accepts a function argument. This is the function that "detects the change and runs your defined logic to fetch updated data."

---

**LINK 2: `https://www.contentstack.com/docs/headless-cms/custom-preview-urls#configure-custom-preview-urls`**

- **Classification:** Vendor documentation (Contentstack)
- **Relevance:** RELEVANT — This documents Custom Preview URLs, which govern how Live Preview resolves preview URLs for content types. This bears directly on the ticket's scope (previewing specific pages like MX Homepage, Discover Article page) and on the comment thread's concern about whether links/navigation works in Live Preview.
- **Fetched:** Yes (HTTP 200, truncated at 8,192 of 16,847 chars — the first ~80% was read)

**Key findings:**

1. **Custom Preview URLs are configured entirely in the Contentstack UI**, not in application code:
   > *"Go to your stack and click 'Settings'. Navigate to Visual Experience and select the Preview URL tab. Enable the Custom Preview URL toggle."*

   URL patterns use `{{...}}` placeholders referencing entry fields, context variables, and taxonomy values:
   > *`{{entry.slug}}, {{entry.title}}, {{entry.uid}}`*
   > *`{{environment}}, {{locale}}, {{branch}}, {{contentTypeUid}}`*

2. **The default (non-custom) preview URL pattern is fixed:**
   > *"`<environment base URL> + <entry's native URL field>`"*
   
   The doc states: *"This approach works for simple scenarios but does not support advanced routing setups."* — This is relevant to the ticket's concern about whether links work in Live Preview; complex routing requires Custom Preview URL configuration.

3. **Plan availability note:**
   > *"Custom Preview URLs is available on select plans. Contact Contentstack support for more information."*

4. **Visual Editor integration** — Custom Preview URLs also resolve in the Visual Editor (the new name/evolution of the "Visual experience" tab referenced in the ticket):
   > *"Custom Preview URLs resolve in Visual Editor the same way they do in Live Preview. After you configure a pattern under Settings → Visual Experience → Preview URL, Visual Editor uses that configuration to resolve entry URLs on the canvas. No additional setup is required."*

---

**UNREACHABLE / NOT VERIFIED:**

- **Codeline architecture (App Router vs. Pages Router, CSR vs. SSR):** Could not confirm. `package.json` did not contain "contentstack" (the SDK may not yet be installed, or may be under a different package name). `next.config.source.ts` and `next.config.js` exist but were not read due to tool budget. **This is critical: if the project uses SSR or Pages Router, Link 1's guide is the wrong architectural variant.**
- **Link 2 truncated:** The last ~20% of the Custom Preview URLs page was not read (taxonomy-driven URLs section onward). The read portion covers configuration, pattern syntax, use cases, and Visual Editor integration.

